{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PHASE 1 RCA — same-change Verify cancellation regression (2026-08-14)\n\nProblem\nA replacement Gerrit Verify run for the same change can remain pending while the superseded run continues consuming runners. Run 31834896849 waited 25m10s before its first job and finished in 57m29s.\n\nProven root cause\nCommit 6f4123678c (docs-only routing) added job-level always() predicates to every full-route reusable job in .github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml (current lines 222, 234, 251, 267, 281, 295, and 350). On a superseding patchset, the predecessor prerequisites remain successful and route=full, so those predicates re-evaluate true. GitHub therefore does not send cancellation to the stale jobs. The stale run keeps the same-change concurrency slot until its test matrix finishes naturally, so the replacement has no job to queue.\n\nDiscriminating proof\n- Across 12 same-change overlaps in the latest 100 completed runs, successor first-job creation occurred within 0–1s of predecessor termination. The initial hold is concurrency admission, not runner acquisition.\n- In the post-6f412 outlier, predecessor 31834162663 kept four pytest jobs alive after successor creation. They ended as ordinary held-out assertion failures at +14s, +49s, +214s, and +1,499s; none concluded cancelled. The predecessor consumed 1,785 runner-seconds after the replacement existed.\n- The macOS log reports 1 failed, 13,173 passed in 1,852.36s and no cancellation message. The successor clear job was created only after the predecessor vote completed.\n- Pre-6f412 controls with the same pytest/xdist command show explicit The operation was canceled messages and cancelled conclusions within 25–104s.\n- Runner scarcity is rejected for this hold: the successor clear job started 3s after it was finally created.\n- A bad concurrency key is rejected: all overlap pairs were the same Gerrit change, and admission unlocked exactly when the predecessor terminated.\n- Pytest signal handling is rejected: pre-6f412 xdist jobs terminate when the cancellation request is actually sent.\n\nAuthority and OSS consensus\nGitHub docs commit 81ade08c, workflow-cancellation.md lines 18–22, explicitly states that running jobs whose if condition remains true are not canceled and names if: always() as the example. The original smoky-flare-slug contract requires new patchsets to auto-cancel prior runs. The docs-only ticket brainy-accused-wrasse requires full fallback on classifier failure, but cancellation is not classifier failure. Home Assistant, Apache Airflow, and Grafana all combine cancellation concurrency with cancellation-aware !cancelled() fan-in/report predicates.\n\nLegitimate remediation\nReplace the full-route job-level always() status check with a cancellation-aware status check while retaining the existing prerequisite and route expression. This preserves full fallback on classifier failure, preserves every successful-patchset job and assertion, and removes only obsolete-patchset work. No prior ticket rejection was found; frowsy-beggarly-mammoth concerns triage wording, not cancellation behavior."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786743212214617001,"uuid":"a74812f6-a2a5-4cdc-bb44-f9d2d703d1c1"}